# 7. Pod Lifecycle

```
Pending

↓

Running

↓

Succeeded OR Failed

↓

Terminating
```

---

## Pending

Pod is being prepared.

Kubernetes is

* Selecting Worker Node
* Downloading Image
* Creating Network
* Preparing Storage

Example

```
kubectl apply

↓

Pending
```

---

## Running

Container starts successfully.

Application is ready.

Example

```
STATUS

Running
```

---

## Succeeded

Used for one-time jobs.

Examples

* Backup
* Report Generation
* Data Migration

Work completed successfully.

```
Backup

↓

Completed

↓

Succeeded
```

---

## Failed

Pod finished with an error.

Example

```
Python Script

↓

Exception

↓

Failed
```

---

## Terminating

When Pod is deleted.

```
Running

↓

kubectl delete pod

↓

Terminating

↓

Deleted
```

---

# 8. Container Crash vs Worker Node Crash

## Case 1

Container crashes

```
Pod

↓

Container ❌
```

kubelet notices.

Container Runtime recreates the container.

Pod remains the same.

---

## Case 2

Worker Node crashes

```
Worker Node ❌

↓

Pod ❌

↓

Container ❌
```

Control Plane schedules a **new Pod** on another healthy Worker Node.

---

# 9. Pod IP Address

Every Pod gets its own IP.

Example

```
React Pod

192.168.1.10

Backend Pod

192.168.1.11

MySQL Pod

192.168.1.12
```

Why?

* Unique network identity
* Easy communication between Pods
* Isolation

---

# 10. kubectl get pods

Command

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

Example

```
NAME        READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE

nginx-pod   1/1     Running   0          27m
```

Meaning

| Column   | Description                           |
| -------- | ------------------------------------- |
| NAME     | Pod Name                              |
| READY    | Running Containers / Total Containers |
| STATUS   | Current Pod State                     |
| RESTARTS | Container Restart Count               |
| AGE      | Pod Age                               |

---

# 11. kubectl describe pod

Command

```bash
kubectl describe pod nginx-pod
```

Important Information

* Pod Name
* Namespace
* Worker Node
* Pod IP
* Container Details
* Events

---

# 12. Events (Pod Creation Flow)

```
Scheduled

↓

Pulling

↓

Pulled

↓

Created

↓

Started
```

Meaning

### Scheduled

Scheduler selected Worker Node.

---

### Pulling

Container Runtime downloads image.

---

### Pulled

Image downloaded successfully.

---

### Created

Container Runtime creates the container.

---

### Started

Container starts.

Pod becomes Running.

---

# 13. Complete Flow

```
You

↓

kubectl apply

↓

API Server

↓

Scheduler

↓

Worker Node Selected

↓

kubelet

↓

Container Runtime

↓

Pull Image

↓

Create Container

↓

Start Container

↓

Pod Running
```

